al  messaging and sort of hanging this  threat  over the population that russia could  turn off the power at the flip of a  switch  other attacks have had different  motivations we caught iran  rifling around the controls at a dam in  the united states  fortunately it was the wrong dam we have  two bowman dams  one of them holds back a tsunami's worth  of water in  oregon and the other holds back a  babbling brook in  new york and they got into the one that  holds back a babbling brook and it was  under repair so it wasn't an actual  threat  but you know who knows what the message  was there  i think it was probably just some sort  of experimentation to see if they could  get into these systems  in case they needed to one day you know  unleash  the locks at that dam um and that's more  of what we're starting to see we're  starting to see  nation state hackers get into these  critical infrastructure systems  not necessarily because they want to do  something immediately  but because we're all planning for some  kind of geopolitical trigger  where we might need to be in a position  to turn off the lights or unleash the  locks at a dam or contaminate the water  supply  but you know each of these attacks  essentially enshrines critical  infrastructure as a legitimate target  for a cyber attack and at the end of the  day it's civilian populations  who would suffer the results of those  attacks so it's yeah  more dangerous this so this is one of my  questions about how this fits into  warfare more broadly because you know  one of the things that's happened over  the last 10 to 15 years is  we we've heard more about and it  actually has moved in the direction of  the idea of a war that's like a boots on  the ground war the likes of which  you know certainly the first gulf war to  a degree  the 2003 iraq war things are moving away  from that drone warfare is certainly  part of it but also so is cyber warfare  but a foreign power for example shutting  off electricity in a large part of the  united states  we understand the chaos that it will  create we understand the damage it can  do  but how would it fit into a broader war  in the sense of what would it be part of  trying to accomplish for the foreign  power that does it  i i'm guessing it's not shut the power  off so they can  steal florida from us and make it part  of some other country there must be  what would be the goal or how would it  fit in yeah it's a really good question  and it's an important question because  you don't want to over hype  the threat right but it is the reality  that russia has been targeting our grid  for several years now a few years ago  the department of homeland security  published a screenshot that literally  showed russian hackers inside  our power plants with their fingers on  the switches that could turn off the  lights  so we know that they have gotten into  these systems which begs the question  what are they doing there you know at  what point will they see some kind of  incentive or motivation to turn off the  lights  right i do think that this is all  battlefield preparation for some  future event whether it's you know a  declaration of war  and this would be one element of that  warfare  turning off the lights or shutting off  the communication systems  in the event that geopolitical tensions  got so  heated that one of these countries felt  the need to do so  we have been hacking into russia's grid  i broke that story with my colleague  david singer a couple years ago  partly as retaliation somewhat for the  election but also for russia's acts and  incursions of our own grid  but we've been doing it more as mutually  assured destruction  i was going to say yeah i was going to  say it reminds me of like  during nuclear weapon development a lot  of people say  what is the circumstance where we would  ever use even 10 of these nukes like  what is that situation and it becomes  this conversation of well you know  if they did it we would we woul