 it's really unregulated space um  it is getting more and more dangerous  particularly as we digitize more of our  infrastructure  and the thing i think people don't  realize is that you know america  might still be the greatest offensive  player in this realm  like i said no one has pulled off  anything close to the attack we pulled  off on iran  more than 10 years ago but we are also  the most targeted we have  more nation states and cyber criminals  lined up at our borders at our systems  knocking on the door getting in in a lot  of cases and planting back doors  and we're the most vulnerable in a lot  of cases because we are so  wired much more so than adversaries like  north korea and iran etc  so on that note i mean in the limited  time we have left  the reaction of some might be well we've  got to pull all of our we  have to go backwards technologically we  have to get a lot of stuff that's  currently networked off of networks uh  silo things etc in in most instances  that's  that's not really a solution to problems  would it be the wrong  the wrong reaction to this no i mean i  think we do  need to step back and think harder about  the defense of these systems eighty  percent  of america's critical infrastructure is  owned and maintained by private  companies and any attempt  to sort of legislate that they have  better security  um has has always failed lobbyists have  always watered it down so any  attempt to secure our critical  infrastructures come in the form of an  executive order  first under obama and then there was one  under trump  but really you know these are all just  setting voluntary  standards for these critical  infrastructure owners to up their  security and  unfortunately a lot of these systems are  owned by small medium-sized businesses  local municipal systems  that simply don't have the security  budgets or resources of a pg  e say here in california so they have to  rely on this outside network of  contractors and supervisors  and the fact that they have to let those  people in remotely creates an  opportunity for hackers so what do we do  you know if we're not going to legislate  that these companies have to up their  security what do you do and i think  one of the things that we need to start  talking about is  creating better market incentives for  these companies to up their security  you know giving tax credits maybe to  companies who  use two-factor authentication and have  all of their software up to date and  pass with flying colors on a penetration  testing  exam um or perhaps it's time to exact  penalties on companies who are not  updating and patching their systems and  allowing anyone to remote in  so i do actually think it's a really  important question to ask ourselves okay  if we're not going to let the government  into these private systems  if we're going to rely on the market  well the market  has always led us down a path of further  vulnerability because  security has no return return on  investment  yeah it's the deep water horizon the 500  000  valve that if you install it everywhere  you protect but it's unlikely you'll  need it everywhere and then they install  it nowhere at all that's such a great  analogy  i hadn't thought of that but that is the  perfect analogy and so we really need to  have these conversations around  how to lock up these systems and  you know there's been some conversations  about should we have a digital geneva  convention  should we set some kind of international  norms of behavior  where we would agree not to hack  russia's power grid and they would agree  not to hack ours or hospitals or  elections  the problem is we the united states will  never agree  to those norms because one  we are still we still have a huge  offensive advantage  but also too we don't trust that our  adversaries would hold to those  agreements  because they outsource many of their  attacks  to cyber criminals to  contractors and front companies the  attacks that we conduct  